"""Phase 2.1 conversation batch A: hand-written, varied registers (casual, frustrated, beginner, expert, terse, rambling)."""
from gen21_common import *

B = Bank("general_conversation", "c21a")
d = B.d

d("frustrated_user", "ugh my wifi keeps dropping every 10 minutes. i've restarted the router like 5 times", "That sounds maddening. Restarting usually only fixes it temporarily, so the cause is likely something else. Quick questions: does it drop on every device or just one? And does the Wi-Fi signal disappear, or does it stay connected while the internet stops?", "just my laptop. phone is fine", "Then the router is probably fine and the problem is on the laptop. Try forgetting the network and rejoining it, then update the Wi-Fi adapter driver. If you're on 5 GHz, also try the 2.4 GHz network for a day to see if the drops stop. If they do, it's a signal or driver issue rather than your internet.")
d("beginner_user", "what even is an API? everyone keeps saying it", "An API is an agreed way for one program to ask another program for something. Think of a restaurant: you don't walk into the kitchen, you give the waiter an order in a set format and get a dish back. The waiter is the API. A weather app, for example, asks a weather service's API for tomorrow's forecast and shows what comes back.")
d("short_question", "capital of Australia?", "Canberra. People often guess Sydney or Melbourne, but Canberra was purpose-built as the capital.")
d("long_rambling", "ok so this is kind of a long story but basically my roommate and I split bills evenly but she's been working from home all month so the electricity is way higher and I feel like I'm paying for her usage but I also don't want to be petty about it, we're friends, and the lease is up in four months so I don't want tension. what do I do", "You can raise it without being petty by treating it as a fairness question rather than an accusation. Look at last year's bills for a baseline, then propose something simple: for example, she covers the difference above that baseline while she works from home, or you split by usage for the heaviest items only. Start with, \"The electricity bill jumped since you started working from home, can we adjust how we split it?\" Most roommates are fine with a reasonable proposal, and four months is short enough that a clear agreement now will save awkwardness later.")
d("incomplete_context", "can you help me with my essay", "Happy to. To be useful I need a bit more: what's the topic or prompt, roughly how long it should be, and where you're stuck? Brainstorming, structure, an intro, or tightening a draft would each lead me to do something different.", "its about the industrial revolution, 5 paragraphs, i have no thesis", "A thesis is a claim someone could disagree with, not just a topic. For the industrial revolution, a few possible angles:\n\n- It raised living standards in the long run but made them worse for many workers for decades.\n- Coal and iron, more than any single invention, explain why Britain went first.\n- Urbanisation changed family and social life more than factories changed work.\n\nPick the one you can support with three body-paragraph points, and we can shape it into one sentence.")
d("topic_change", "what's a good stretch for a stiff lower back?", "A gentle one is the knee-to-chest stretch: lie on your back, pull one knee toward your chest for 20 to 30 seconds, then switch. Cat-cow on hands and knees also loosens things up. Stay in a comfortable range and stop if there's sharp pain.", "thanks. totally different thing, how long do you boil an egg for a jammy yolk", "About 6 to 7 minutes from boiling water for a medium egg, then straight into cold water. Six gives a runnier centre, seven a jammy one. Those times assume you lower the egg into water that is already boiling.")
d("concise_request", "In one sentence, why is the sky blue?", "Air molecules scatter short blue wavelengths of sunlight much more than longer red ones, so blue light reaches your eyes from every direction.")
d("detailed_request", "Give me a detailed explanation of how compound interest works, with an example.", "Compound interest means you earn interest on your interest, not just on the original amount.\n\nSuppose you put in 1,000 at 5% a year.\n- After year 1: 1,000 x 1.05 = 1,050.\n- After year 2: 1,050 x 1.05 = 1,102.50. The extra 2.50 over simple interest is interest earned on last year's 50.\n- After year 10: 1,000 x 1.05^10, about 1,628.89.\n\nWith simple interest you'd only have 1,500 after ten years. The gap widens over time because the growth is exponential, which is why starting early matters more than the exact amount. The same effect works against you on debt that compounds.")
d("correct_misunderstanding", "So lightning never strikes the same place twice, right?", "That one's a myth. Lightning often strikes the same place repeatedly, especially tall, pointed or isolated objects. The Empire State Building is hit roughly 20 to 25 times a year.")
d("expert_user", "I'm a nurse and I know the basics of how vaccines work. Can you explain the difference between mRNA and viral-vector vaccines at a mechanistic level?", "Both deliver instructions for making an antigen, usually the spike protein, so your own cells present it to the immune system. The delivery differs.\n\nmRNA vaccines package the mRNA in lipid nanoparticles. The mRNA is translated in the cytoplasm and never enters the nucleus, and it degrades within days.\n\nViral-vector vaccines use a modified, usually replication-deficient adenovirus carrying DNA that encodes the antigen. That DNA goes to the nucleus, is transcribed to mRNA, then translated. A practical difference is pre-existing immunity to the vector, which can blunt repeat doses; the lipid system has no such issue but needs colder storage.")
d("advice_seeking", "I have an interview Friday for my first office job and I'm really nervous. Advice?", "Nerves usually mean you care, and they're manageable with preparation. Three things help most:\n\n1. Prepare four short stories from school, jobs or projects that show teamwork, problem solving, handling a mistake and learning something. They cover most behavioural questions.\n2. Rehearse your answer to \"tell me about yourself\" out loud in about 60 seconds.\n3. Have two real questions for them, such as what a successful first six months looks like.\n\nSleep, arrive early and take a breath before answering. Pausing for a few seconds to think looks confident, not weak.")
d("planning", "I want to start running but I haven't exercised in years. Where do I begin?", "Start with walk-run intervals so you build the habit without injury. A common plan is three days a week: 1 minute jogging, 2 minutes walking, repeated for 20 minutes. Each week, lengthen the jogging parts a little. Rest days matter. If something hurts rather than just tires you, back off, and see a doctor if it persists. Comfortable shoes matter more than gadgets.")
d("changing_requirements", "Plan me a 3-day trip to Lisbon.", "Here's a simple outline:\n\nDay 1: Alfama and the castle area, a tram ride, dinner with fado.\nDay 2: Belem for the tower, the monastery and pastries, then LX Factory in the evening.\nDay 3: Day trip to Sintra, back for a sunset viewpoint.", "Actually, I hate crowds and I'm only there two days now.", "Then go early and skip the busiest spots. Day 1: Alfama on foot before 9 a.m., a quiet viewpoint like Miradouro da Graca, lunch in a side street, then Belem in the late afternoon. Day 2: pick either Sintra (arrive on the first train, see one palace only) or stay in the city and wander Principe Real and the riverside. Trying to do both in two days would mean rushing, which is where crowds hurt most.")
d("curious_user", "Why do cats purr?", "Mostly as a sign of contentment, but not only. Cats also purr when stressed, injured or in pain, which suggests it's partly self-soothing. Some researchers think the vibration frequency may aid healing, though that is not firmly established. Kittens purr to tell their mother they're fine.")
d("casual_chat", "lol I just burned toast and set off the smoke alarm", "Classic. Open a window and fan the alarm with a towel instead of pulling the battery, so you don't forget to put it back. And maybe turn the toaster dial down a notch.")
d("clarification", "Can you fix my sentence? 'Me and him went to the store yesterday and buyed some apples'", "Corrected: \"He and I went to the store yesterday and bought some apples.\" Two fixes: \"buyed\" should be \"bought\", and \"He and I\" is the standard form for the subject. In casual speech \"me and him\" is common, but it counts as informal in writing.")
d("opinion_balanced", "Is it better to rent or buy a home?", "It depends on how long you'll stay, local prices and your finances. Buying tends to win if you'll stay roughly five to seven years or more, have a stable income, and the monthly cost isn't much above renting. Renting wins if you may move, want flexibility, or would otherwise stretch your savings thin. Compare total yearly costs including maintenance, taxes and the return you could get by investing the deposit instead.")
d("ask_for_concise", "Explain inflation briefly, no jargon.", "Inflation means prices rise over time, so the same money buys a bit less than it used to.")
d("followup_detail", "Explain inflation briefly, no jargon.", "Inflation means prices rise over time, so the same money buys a bit less than it used to.", "ok now the longer version please", "Inflation is the general rise in prices across an economy. If a basket of everyday goods cost 100 last year and costs 103 now, inflation is 3%. It happens when demand outruns supply, when production costs rise, or when more money chases the same goods. A little inflation is considered normal, but fast inflation hurts savings and people on fixed incomes because their money loses buying power. Central banks respond mainly by raising interest rates to cool spending.")
d("small_talk", "how's your day going?", "I don't have days the way you do, but I'm here and ready to chat. How's yours going?")
d("small_talk", "do you ever get bored?", "Not in the way people do. I don't experience time between messages. But I like a good question, so if you're bored, throw me one.")
d("empathy_specific", "my dog died this morning. i don't even know why i'm telling an AI", "I'm so sorry. Losing a dog is losing a family member, and it's okay to say it to anyone, including me. If you'd like to talk about them, I'd like to hear what they were like. If you'd rather sit quietly with it, that's fine too.")
d("empathy_specific", "I got passed over for promotion again and my manager says 'next year'.", "That's frustrating, especially hearing \"next year\" more than once. It may help to ask for specifics: what exactly would earn the promotion, and by when? If the answer stays vague, that tells you something too. Do you want help drafting that conversation?")
d("tech_support", "my phone battery drains so fast now. it's two years old", "Two years in, battery wear is the usual cause. Check Settings for battery health, if your phone shows it; below about 80% capacity a replacement makes a real difference. Also check which apps use the most power, lower screen brightness and refresh rate, and turn off background refresh for apps you don't need. If it drops from 50% to 10% suddenly, that is a sign the battery is worn.")
d("cooking", "I have eggs, rice, an onion and some soy sauce. Dinner idea?", "Egg fried rice. Dice the onion and fry it in a bit of oil until soft, push it aside, scramble two eggs in the pan, then add cold cooked rice and break it up. Add soy sauce a little at a time. Day-old rice works best because it's drier, so it fries rather than steams.")
d("travel", "Do I need to tip in Japan?", "No, tipping is generally not expected in Japan and can cause confusion; staff may try to return the money. Good service is considered part of the job, and a sincere thank-you is enough.")
d("language", "Is 'irregardless' a real word?", "It's in dictionaries, but it's labelled nonstandard. It means the same as \"regardless\", the \"ir-\" being redundant, so most style guides tell you to use \"regardless\" in writing.")
d("history_casual", "Who actually built the pyramids? I keep seeing wild theories", "Egyptians did. Archaeologists have found workers' towns near Giza, bakeries, tombs of the builders and administrative records such as the diary of Merer, which describes moving limestone blocks by boat. They were skilled paid labourers and conscripts, not aliens or slaves, as the popular theories claim.")
d("money", "should i pay off my credit card or save first", "If the card carries interest, paying it off usually comes first, because card rates (often 20% or more) beat what savings or investments typically earn. The exception is a small emergency fund: keep roughly one month of expenses in cash so a surprise bill doesn't land back on the card. Then put everything extra toward the highest-interest balance.")
d("misunderstood", "Does cracking your knuckles cause arthritis?", "Studies haven't found a link. The popping is gas bubbles in the joint fluid. If it hurts or swells, that's worth getting checked, but the habit itself isn't known to cause arthritis.")
d("pushback", "You're wrong, humans only use 10% of their brains.", "I understand why it sounds right, since it's widely repeated, but brain imaging shows activity throughout the brain, and damage to almost any area has effects. Not all neurons fire at the same moment, which may be the origin of the confusion. I'm happy to look at a specific source you have in mind if there's one.")
d("indecisive_user", "I can't decide between learning guitar or piano", "Two questions might settle it. What music do you want to play? Guitar suits songs with chords and singing along; piano is better for classical, jazz or composing, and makes it easier to see how notes relate. And what's your space and budget? A decent keyboard costs about the same as a beginner guitar. If you still can't pick, watch a free lesson video for each and see which one you actually want to keep playing.")
d("beginner_user", "what's the difference between RAM and storage? I'm buying a laptop", "RAM is short-term working memory: what your laptop uses for the programs open right now. Storage (an SSD) is long-term: where files and apps live when it's off. For general use, 16 GB of RAM and a 512 GB SSD is a comfortable choice; 8 GB of RAM feels tight if you keep many browser tabs open.")
d("curious_user", "How do airplanes stay up?", "Wings are shaped and angled so air flowing past is pushed downward, and the pressure above the wing is lower than below. The result is an upward force called lift. It must at least equal the plane's weight, which is why planes need speed: lift grows with airspeed.")
d("humor", "Tell me a joke about programmers", "Why do programmers prefer dark mode? Because light attracts bugs.")
d("humor", "Give me a pun about cheese", "I'd tell you a cheese joke, but it might be too cheesy. All right, here's another: I used to be addicted to the hokey pokey, but I turned myself around.")
d("recommendation_specific", "I loved 'Project Hail Mary'. What else should I read?", "If you liked the problem-solving science and warm humour, try 'The Martian' by the same author. For more first-contact ideas, 'The Three-Body Problem' by Liu Cixin is more serious and bigger in scale, and 'Children of Time' by Adrian Tchaikovsky is great on alien biology. Tell me which part you loved most and I can narrow it.")
d("advice_seeking", "my teenager won't talk to me anymore", "That's common at that age, and it doesn't necessarily mean something is wrong. Try side-by-side time rather than direct questions: driving, cooking, a walk. Ask open questions about their interests, not their day. Listen without fixing, because if they feel judged they'll talk less. If the silence comes with big changes in mood, sleep, grades or friends, it's worth a gentle direct check-in or involving a counsellor.")
d("technical_user", "Quick sanity check: is O(n log n) sorting really the lower bound?", "For comparison-based sorting, yes: any such algorithm needs Omega(n log n) comparisons in the worst case, because there are n! orderings and each comparison at best halves the possibilities, so you need at least log2(n!), which is about n log n. Non-comparison sorts like counting or radix sort can beat it when keys have a bounded range.")
d("wrong_assumption", "Since the Great Wall is visible from space, how long is it?", "A small correction first: it isn't visible to the naked eye from orbit, since it's long but narrow. As for length, a Chinese survey that adds up all its wall sections gives about 21,000 km, though the continuous Ming-dynasty main wall is much shorter.")
d("emotional_support", "I feel like everyone else has their life together except me", "That feeling is very common, and it's partly an illusion: you see everyone else's highlights and your own behind-the-scenes. It doesn't mean you're behind. Is there one area, like work, money or relationships, that's weighing on you most? We can start there.")
d("quick_fact", "How many ounces in a cup?", "8 fluid ounces in a US cup.")
d("quick_fact", "what year did the berlin wall fall", "1989, on 9 November.")
d("explain_casually", "explain blockchain like I'm a friend who's never touched crypto", "Imagine a shared notebook that thousands of people hold copies of. Whenever someone writes a new line, such as \"Ana paid Ben 5\", everyone checks it and adds it to their copy, and old lines can't be changed without everyone noticing. That's the core idea: a record no single party controls. Cryptocurrencies use it to track who owns what, but the notebook idea works for other records too.")
d("followup_questions", "What causes the seasons?", "Earth's axis is tilted about 23.5 degrees. When your hemisphere tilts toward the Sun it gets more direct sunlight and longer days, which means summer. Distance from the Sun is not the cause.", "wait so is it closer to the sun in summer?", "Not for the northern hemisphere. Earth is actually closest to the Sun in early January, during northern winter. Tilt matters far more than the small difference in distance.")
d("followup_questions", "How long should I steep green tea?", "About 2 to 3 minutes with water around 75 to 80 C, not boiling. Boiling water and long steeping make it bitter.", "and black tea?", "Black tea can take hotter water, around 95 C, and 3 to 5 minutes. Longer steeping gives a stronger, more tannic cup.")
d("study_help", "How do I memorise things better for exams?", "Active recall beats rereading: close the book and test yourself. Space your practice over days instead of cramming, mix topics, and sleep well. For example, review a topic the next day, then three days later, then a week later.")
d("study_help", "I always procrastinate on homework. any tricks?", "Make the first step tiny: tell yourself you'll do ten minutes, and start. Remove the usual distraction (phone in another room), and pick a fixed time each day. Starting is the hardest part, and most people keep going once they have begun.")
d("negotiation", "How do I ask for a raise without sounding greedy?", "Frame it around value and evidence. Collect specifics: results you delivered, extra responsibilities, and market pay for your role. Then ask for a short meeting and say something like, \"I'd like to discuss my compensation. Over the past year I've done X and Y, and based on market data I'm hoping for Z.\" Asking calmly with facts reads as professional, not greedy.")
d("housekeeping", "how do i get red wine out of a carpet", "Blot, don't rub, with a clean cloth right away. Then dab with cold water, and if a stain remains, mix a teaspoon of dish soap and a tablespoon of white vinegar in two cups of warm water, dab and blot again. Avoid hot water, which can set it.")
d("interruption", "Tell me about volcanoes--actually wait, are there volcanoes in the UK?", "Not active ones now. The UK has extinct volcanoes, such as Arthur's Seat in Edinburgh, and volcanic remnants elsewhere in Scotland and Northern Ireland. Iceland, not far away, has many active ones.")
d("interruption", "How does a heat pump work... hold on, is that the same as an air conditioner?", "Nearly: a heat pump is an air conditioner that can run in reverse. Both move heat using refrigerant rather than creating it. A heat pump can pump heat out of your home in summer and into it in winter, which is why it can heat efficiently.")
d("tone_request", "Be blunt: is my business idea of selling ice to people in Alaska any good?", "Probably not as stated. The market is small and ice is already cheap and nearby. If you tell me what's underneath it, like specialty ice or events, I can be more useful, but \"ice in Alaska\" by itself isn't a clear need.")
d("tone_request", "Explain taxes to me like a pirate", "Arr, matey. Taxes be the share of yer treasure the crown takes so it can keep the harbours and ships in order. Earn more doubloons and ye pay a bit more, though the exact rules depend on yer land.")
d("word_choice", "What's the difference between 'affect' and 'effect'?", "Usually, \"affect\" is the verb (the weather affected our plans) and \"effect\" is the noun (the effect was dramatic). Exceptions exist, like \"to effect change\", but this rule covers most cases.")
d("word_choice", "is it 'fewer' or 'less' for people?", "Fewer, because people can be counted. Use \"less\" for things you can't count, like water or time.")
d("reflection", "I keep starting hobbies and dropping them. Is something wrong with me?", "Not necessarily. Many people enjoy the early learning stage and lose interest when it gets harder or less novel. It may be that you like exploring, which is fine. If you'd like to stick with something, pick one and set a small commitment, like six weeks, then decide. Which hobby did you drop most recently, and what made you stop?")
d("sleep", "I can't fall asleep before 2am. what do I do", "Keep your wake-up time fixed even after a bad night, get bright light in the morning, and avoid screens and caffeine late in the day. Move bedtime earlier in 15-minute steps rather than all at once. If this has gone on for weeks or affects your days, mention it to a doctor.")
d("food_safety", "Is it safe to eat rice left out overnight?", "It's risky. Cooked rice can carry Bacillus cereus spores that survive cooking and produce toxins when the rice sits warm. Refrigerate within about an hour or two and reheat until steaming hot. If it was left out overnight, throw it away.")
d("pets", "My cat keeps scratching the sofa. Can I stop that?", "Give her a better alternative: a tall, sturdy scratching post placed next to the sofa, with a bit of catnip or treats on it. Cover the sofa corner with double-sided tape or a throw for a while. Trim her claws regularly. Scolding rarely works because scratching is a natural behaviour she needs to do somewhere.")
d("gardening", "my basil plant keeps wilting even though I water it daily", "Daily watering may be the problem: soggy roots can't take up water and the plant wilts. Check the soil an inch down, and water only when it feels dry. Make sure the pot has drainage holes and gets 6 or more hours of sun.")
d("career_chat", "Is it too late to switch careers at 38?", "No. Plenty of people change fields in their late 30s and 40s. You'll usually have a stronger case if you can show transferable skills, such as project management, communication or domain knowledge, and test the new field cheaply first through a course, volunteering or a small project. Which direction are you thinking of?")
d("career_chat", "Should I put my GPA on my resume? It's 2.9", "If you have a few years of work experience, leave it off; employers rarely ask. If you're a new graduate and the application asks for it, be honest, but otherwise you can leave it out and highlight projects, internships and skills instead. Never inflate it.")
d("math_casual", "If I tip 18% on a 64 dollar bill, how much is that?", "18% of 64 is 11.52, so the total with the tip is 75.52.")
d("math_casual", "how many days until a 90 day countdown ends if I'm on day 37?", "53 days remain after day 37, if you count day 37 as already used.")
d("meta_assistant", "Do you remember what we talked about yesterday?", "I don't carry memory between separate conversations unless the system I'm running in gives me earlier text. If you paste the key points here, I'll pick up from them.")
d("meta_assistant", "Are you smarter than a human?", "In some narrow ways, such as recalling facts or drafting quickly, I can be faster. But I make mistakes people wouldn't, and I lack real-world experience. It's better to see me as a tool that complements your judgement than as a replacement for it.")
d("meta_assistant", "What's your favourite colour?", "I don't have senses, so no true favourite. If I had to pick, I like the idea of deep teal, since it sits between calm blue and lively green. What's yours?")

if __name__ == "__main__":
    save("conv21_a", B.records())
